patches.behavior:
State per loop index: `{ seeded: false, prev: Value, out: Value, queue: { due: number, value: Value }[] }`.

Each frame:
```
const v = ctx.input("value");
const d = safeDuration(ctx.input("duration")); // NaN or ±Infinity → 0 with one warning per restart; negative → 0
const style = ctx.input("style");
if (!state.seeded) {
  state.seeded = true;
  // An upstream pulse on the first frame is an event, not a starting state.
  if (typeParam === "boolean" && ctx.isPulseSource("value") && ctx.pulsed("value")) { state.prev = false; state.out = false; }
  else { state.prev = v; state.out = v; }
}
if (!equal(v, state.prev)) {
  const dir = direction(state.prev, v); // +1 rise, -1 fall, 0 for types without an order
  const wait = d > 0 && (style === "always" || dir === 0 ||
    (style === "whenIncreasing" && dir > 0) || (style === "whenDecreasing" && dir < 0));
  if (wait) state.queue.push({ due: ctx.time + d, value: v });
  else { state.queue.length = 0; state.out = v; } // pass through and cancel every pending change
  state.prev = v;
}
while (state.queue.length > 0 && ctx.time >= state.queue[0].due - 1e-6) state.out = state.queue.shift().value;
if (state.queue.length > 0) ctx.requestNextFrame();
ctx.output("output", state.out);
```

- Change detection: `equal` is value equality. Numbers, indices, booleans, text, and enum keys compare with `===`; vectors and colors compare component-wise; JSON compares deeply; layer and media references compare by id. Only changes enter the queue, so a steady input costs nothing.
- Direction: `number` and `index` compare numerically, and `boolean` orders false < true. Every other variant returns 0, which behaves like Always (Origami documents the same rule).
- Queue model: time-stamped, never frame-counted (CONVENTIONS.md §18.5). The output at time t is the input as it was at t − Duration. Entries release in order: when Duration shrinks, a newer entry never overtakes an older one; it releases on the first frame when it and every older entry are due.
- Pulses: there are no pulse ports, but Value may receive a pulse, which arrives as a one-frame `true` on the boolean variant. With When Decreasing, the rise passes through and the fall one frame later waits Duration, so the output stays true for Duration after the latest pulse. Another pulse during the hold passes through, cancels the pending fall, and restarts the hold. With Always, a pulse replays as a one-frame blip Duration later. A pulse into Value is intended: the "pulse wired into a state input" diagnostic must not fire for this port.
- Frame 0: the output starts at the first input value, with nothing queued (gap-fill R1), so a Switch that starts on doesn't animate in. Exception: when Value is driven by a pulse output (`ctx.isPulseSource("value")`, boolean variant) and that pulse arrives on the first evaluation, it counts as a change from false. A When Prototype Starts pulse is therefore delayed like any other, and a loop of durations staggers entrances that each launch on time. A held boolean that is already true on frame 0 still seeds as true.
- Loops: per-index state. `value`, `duration`, and `style` may each be loops (a loop of durations staggers items). New indices seed as on frame 0; removed indices drop their queues.
- Retargeting: each queued change keeps the due time computed when it was queued, so editing Duration or Style affects only later changes.
- Edge cases: Duration 0 or negative passes every change through immediately and clears the queue. A NaN on the number variant is treated as 0 with one warning per restart. The queue is capped at 10,000 entries per index; past the cap, the oldest entry releases immediately and one warning is logged per restart.
- Timing precision: an entry releases on the first frame whose `ctx.time` is at or past its due time, with 1e-6 s tolerance, so at 60 fps a 0.5 s delay lands exactly 30 frames later.
- Restart and dispose: queues clear, and the next evaluation seeds again.
- Coming from Origami: the legacy implementation counted frames (`lround(Duration × fps)` with a smoothed fps) and output nothing until enough history existed. The time-stamped queue gives the same result at 60 fps without the startup gap and stays correct at 120 fps and under frame drops. Origami's output label is Value; Sonobe's key is `output`.

State per loop index: `{ seeded: false, held: Value }`.

Delay One Frame is a one-frame register: its output never depends on this frame's input.

**Ordering and cycles.** Outside a cycle, the compiler orders this patch after its upstream as usual. When a cycle passes through a `delay1`, the compiler breaks the cycle at the edge into that patch's `value` port, so the cycle has exactly one frame of latency, never two. A cycle that contains a `delay1` never raises the zero-latency cycle diagnostic (gap-fill R3).

Each frame:
```
if (ctx.isFeedback("value")) {
  // The back-edge read already returns the upstream's previous-frame value (ARCHITECTURE.md §5.2),
  // which is exactly one frame late, so pass it through. On frame 0 there is no previous frame:
  // the read returns the value port's default (the variant's zero value).
  ctx.output("output", ctx.input("value"));
} else {
  const v = ctx.input("value");
  if (!state.seeded) {
    state.seeded = true;
    state.held = typeParam === "boolean" && ctx.isPulseSource("value") && ctx.pulsed("value") ? false : v;
  }
  ctx.output("output", state.held);
  if (!equal(state.held, v)) ctx.requestNextFrame(); // the output will change next frame
  state.held = v;
}
```

- Engine: the compiler picks the back-edges (every edge into a `delay1` first), and `ctx.isFeedback("value")` reports that choice. The engine currently evaluates `delay1` with a built-in copy of this evaluator.
- Pulses: no pulse ports. A pulse on Value comes out one frame later as a one-frame `true`.
- Frame 0: the output equals the first input, so `value − previous` is 0 on frame 0 and nothing spikes (gap-fill R1). Exception: an upstream pulse (`ctx.isPulseSource`) on the first frame seeds `false`, so the pulse appears one frame later instead of lasting two frames. In a feedback cycle the output on frame 0 is the port default, which is how an accumulator starts from 0.
- Loops: per-index `held`; the output length follows the input loop. New indices seed on their first evaluation; removed indices drop their state.
- Time: defined per frame, not per second (CONVENTIONS.md §16.5 exception). One frame is about 16.7 ms at 60 fps and 8.3 ms at 120 fps. `ctx.dt` is not used.
- Edge cases: values pass through unchanged, with no clamping. Changing `typeParam` resets the register.
- Muted: Value passes straight through with no delay. The compiler keeps the back-edge it chose, so muting a `delay1` inside a cycle never creates a zero-latency cycle.
- Restart and dispose: state clears, and the next evaluation seeds again.
- Coming from Origami: Origami returns the port default on frame 0 and the real input from frame 1, a documented quirk that makes Velocity jump at startup. Sonobe seeds from the first value; an importer's Origami-compatibility mode may reproduce the quirk (gap-fill R1). Origami's port labels are Input and Output.

State per loop index: `{ running: false, elapsed: 0, done: false }`.

Each frame:
```
const d = safeDuration(ctx.input("duration")); // NaN or ±Infinity → 0 with one warning per restart; negative → 0
let finished = false;
if (ctx.pulsed("reset")) { state.running = false; state.elapsed = 0; state.done = false; }
else if (ctx.pulsed("start")) { state.running = true; state.elapsed = 0; state.done = false; }
else if (state.running) state.elapsed += ctx.dt;
if (state.running && state.elapsed >= d - 1e-6) {
  state.running = false;
  state.done = true;
  finished = true;
}
if (state.running) ctx.requestNextFrame();
ctx.output("done", state.done);
ctx.output("progress", state.done ? 1 : d > 0 ? Math.min(state.elapsed / d, 1) : 0);
if (finished) ctx.pulse("finished");
```

- Pulses: `start` and `reset`. Same-frame precedence: `reset` > `start`, and reset never starts the timer. Pulses on consecutive frames each count, so a Start on every frame keeps restarting the timer.
- Timing: time accrues from the frame after Start. Done turns on and Finished fires on the first frame where `ctx.time − startTime ≥ duration`, with 1e-6 s tolerance. At 60 fps a 1 s wait completes exactly 60 frames after the Start frame, and results match at 120 fps.
- Frame 0: idle, so `done` is false and `progress` is 0. A Start pulse on frame 0 (from When Prototype Starts) starts the timer on frame 0.
- Retargeting: Start while running restarts from 0, with no Finished pulse for the interrupted run. Start while done clears Done on that frame and restarts. Duration is read every frame: shortening it below the elapsed time completes on that frame, and lengthening it extends the run. After completion, Duration changes don't affect Done or Progress.
- Edge cases: Duration 0 or negative completes on the Start frame itself: Done is true, Progress is 1, and Finished fires that frame.
- Loops: per-index timers. `start`, `reset`, and `duration` may each be loops; one Start with a loop of durations gives staggered completions. New indices start idle; removed indices drop their state.
- Muted: `done` is false, `progress` is 0, and `finished` never fires. (The default bypass would pass Duration into Progress, which is meaningless.)
- Restart and dispose: every index returns to idle.
- Coming from Origami: the latched boolean matches Origami's documented output and the legacy Timer macro (gap-fill C8, R7). Progress, Finished, and Reset are Sonobe additions. Origami's output label is Output; Sonobe's key is `done`.

State per loop index: `{ elapsed: 0, active: false }` (`active` means Enabled was on at the previous evaluation).

Each frame:
```
const interval = safeDuration(ctx.input("interval")); // NaN or ±Infinity → 0 with one warning per restart; negative → 0
const enabled = ctx.input("enabled");
if (ctx.pulsed("reset")) {
  state.elapsed = 0;
} else if (enabled && state.active) {
  state.elapsed += ctx.dt;
  const ticks = interval > 0 ? Math.floor((state.elapsed + 1e-6) / interval) : 1;
  if (ticks >= 1) {
    ctx.pulse("tick");
    state.elapsed = interval > 0 ? Math.max(0, state.elapsed - ticks * interval) : 0;
  }
}
state.active = enabled;
if (enabled) ctx.requestNextFrame();
```

- Pulses: `reset` is the only pulse input. It restarts the countdown on that frame and suppresses a tick that frame. `tick` lasts one frame. At most one tick fires per frame per index: when one frame spans several intervals (a tiny Interval or a long frame), the extra ticks are dropped and the remainder carries over, so the rhythm doesn't drift.
- Frame 0: no tick. The first tick lands on the first frame at or after `interval` seconds, which is frame 60 at 60 fps for a 1 s interval. When Prototype Starts covers startup.
- Retargeting: Interval is read every frame and compared with the time since the last tick. Shortening it below that time ticks on that frame; lengthening it postpones the next tick.
- Disabled: no ticks, and `elapsed` is kept, so ticking resumes mid-interval. No time accrues on the frame Enabled changes, following the legacy stopwatch convention (gap-fill C9). Pulse Reset in the same frame as enabling to start a full interval.
- Edge cases: an Interval of 0 or below one frame ticks on every active frame, but not on the first frame the patch is active.
- Loops: per-index metronomes; a loop of intervals gives independent rhythms. New indices start fresh, with their first tick one interval after they appear. Removed indices drop their state.
- Determinism: time comes only from `ctx.dt`, never from `setInterval` or wall-clock timers, so simulation and the live viewer agree.
- Restart and dispose: `elapsed` returns to 0.
- Coming from Origami: the legacy build is a square-wave LFO followed by rising-edge detection (semantics.md §5.2), which gives one pulse per period with the first at one period, matching this timing. Origami's Frequency port is `interval`, and its unnamed output is `tick`.

State per loop index: `{ time: 0, frame: 0 }` (the last values output while enabled).

Each frame:
```
if (ctx.input("enabled")) {
  state.time = ctx.time;
  state.frame = ctx.frame;
  ctx.requestNextFrame();
}
ctx.output("time", state.time);
ctx.output("frame", state.frame);
```

- Clock source: `ctx.time` and `ctx.frame`, the runtime's prototype clock. In the live viewer each frame advances time by the real frame interval, capped at 64 ms, so after a stall Time runs behind a wall clock. In simulation time advances exactly 1/fps per frame. Never read `Date.now` or `performance.now`.
- Frame 0: Time is 0 and Frame is 0.
- Disabled: the outputs hold their last values (0 and 0 when disabled from the start). Re-enabling jumps them to the current `ctx.time` and `ctx.frame`; nothing accumulates while disabled.
- Loops: a loop of `enabled` values gives one held pair per index; every enabled index reads the same clock. New indices start at 0 until they're enabled.
- Frame rate: `frame` counts evaluated frames, so it depends on the display rate, while `time` doesn't.
- Muted: outputs 0.
- Restart and dispose: held values return to 0, and `ctx.time` and `ctx.frame` restart at 0.
- Coming from Origami: v172 added an Enable port that Origami's docs still omit; it maps to `enabled`, defaulting to true.

State per loop index: `{ running: false, elapsed: 0 }`.

Each frame:
```
if (state.running) state.elapsed += ctx.dt; // the time since the previous frame counts while running
if (ctx.pulsed("reset")) state.elapsed = 0;
if (ctx.pulsed("stop")) state.running = false;
else if (ctx.pulsed("start")) state.running = true;
if (state.running) ctx.requestNextFrame();
ctx.output("time", state.elapsed);
ctx.output("running", state.running);
```

- Pulses: `start`, `stop`, and `reset`. Same-frame precedence: `reset` applies first, then `stop` > `start`. Reset never starts or stops anything by itself: Reset + Start begins again from 0, and Reset + Stop leaves it stopped at 0.
- Timing: time accrues between frames while running. On the Start frame Time doesn't change; one frame later it has grown by `ctx.dt`. The Stop frame still counts the interval since the previous frame, so one run reads exactly `stopTime − startTime`. Results match at 60 and 120 fps.
- Frame 0: stopped at 0. A Start pulse on frame 0 starts the stopwatch on frame 0.
- Retargeting: Start while running and Stop while stopped do nothing. Reset while running zeroes Time on that frame and keeps counting.
- Edge cases: there are no numeric inputs to guard, and Time never goes negative.
- Loops: per-index stopwatches; looped control pulses drive each index independently. New indices start stopped at 0; removed indices drop their state.
- Clock source: `ctx.dt` (the prototype clock, capped at 64 ms per frame in the live viewer), never the wall clock.
- Restart and dispose: every index returns to stopped at 0.
- Coming from Origami: Stop pauses and Start resumes, as in the legacy stopwatch patch (gap-fill C9), which also kept per-index state. The legacy patch skipped time on frames where its On input toggled; Sonobe counts the Stop frame so a run measures exactly stop − start. Origami leaves same-frame ordering undocumented, so this precedence is a Sonobe decision. `running` is new.
